  "reason": "Evidence: The current best is config_043 (multimodal, multiprofile, scvi_n200, resnet50, avgpool, weighted) at 0.678 acc. We've established that multiprofile >> singleprofile (+0.10-0.14) and weighted > concat fusion (+0.046), but we have only tested avgpool as the aggregation mechanism. Uncertainty: It is unknown whether the simple average pooling adequately captures inter-profile interactions, or whether a transformer-based aggregation could yield further gains by learning which profiles are most informative per cell. Question tested: config_023 changes only the aggregation from avgpool to transformer while holding all other settings identical to config_043 (multimodal, multiprofile, scvi_n200, resnet50, weighted), giving a clean single-variable comparison. Why this is the best next experiment: Aggregation is a fundamental architectural choice that determines how multiprofile information is combined—a dimension we have not yet explored. A positive result would open a new improvement axis; a null result would confirm avgpool sufficiency and redirect budget toward encoder choices (scgpt, ViT). This is more informative than, e.g., swapping the RNA encoder, because if aggregation is already suboptimal, encoder improvements may be confounded by the aggregation bottleneck."
